  "text": "Round 1 (8192 elements per tile) ran at 0.0218 ms and round 2 (16384) was slower at 0.0230 ms, so this round tries smaller tiles of 4096. That doubles the grid to 5120 blocks. More, smaller blocks should help the scheduler cover the 148 SMs and reduce the idle tail at the end, which matters for a kernel limited by memory bandwidth.\n\nI also dropped the zero-padding on the loads. Out-of-bounds lanes of a partial last tile are discarded by the clipped store, so the padding value never reaches the output. In any case, n = 20971520 is an exact multiple of 4096, so there is no partial tile here.\n\nThe add stays in fp16, each element is read once and written once in a single pass, and the output is freshly allocated on every call.\n\n```python title=\"impl_cutile.py\"\nimport torch\nimport cuda.tile as ct\n\nConstInt = ct.Constant[int]\n\n# Fixed configuration literals\n_TILE = 4096\n\n_CONFIG = {\"TILE\": _TILE}\n\n\n@ct.kernel\ndef _vector_add_kernel(x, y, out, TILE: ConstInt):\n    bid = ct.bid(0)\n    # Out-of-bounds lanes (only possible in a partial last tile) are dropped\n    # by the bounds-clipped store, so no padding value is needed.\n    a = ct.load(x, (bid,), (TILE,))\n    b = ct.load(y, (bid,), (TILE,))\n    ct.store(out, (bid,), a + b)\n\n\ndef run(x, y):\n    xf = x.view(-1)\n    yf = y.view(-1)\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    of = out.view(-1)\n    n = xf.numel()\n    if n == 0:\n        return out\n    grid = (ct.cdiv(n, _TILE),)\n    ct.launch(torch.cuda.current_stream(), grid, _vector_add_kernel,\n              (xf, yf, of, _TILE))\n    return out\n\n\ndef get_last_config() -> dict:\n    return dict(_CONFIG)\n```",
